WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.300
You can map a single alert to ATT&CK. Now zoom out: across the entire matrix, which techniques could you actually detect — and which would sail through unnoticed?

2
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:16.700
That question turns alert-mapping into a strategic view of your whole detection program.

3
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.570
The common mistake is treating coverage as a single yes-or-no per technique.

4
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:28.820
In reality it breaks into three layers — and a gap can hide at any one of them, even when the others look fine.

5
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.650
Layer one, visibility — do we even collect the log source that would show this technique happening?

6
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:39.910
Layer two, detection — if we have the data, is there a rule that would actually fire on it?

7
00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:48.810
Layer three, response — if a detection fires, can an analyst act on it fast enough to matter? A gap at any layer is a blind spot.

8
00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:55.120
Picture your coverage painted onto the matrix. Green cells are techniques you'd catch.

9
00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:03.210
The red cells are the gaps — and this is the real picture in almost every SOC. Nobody is covered everywhere.

10
00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:15.980
A classic gap: you collect Sysmon process-access data across every endpoint — visibility exists — but nobody ever wrote a rule for LSASS access. The data is there; the detection isn't.

11
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:21.780
And this isn't just a manager's job. Front-line analysts spot gaps first.

12
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:31.880
When you think "we have no way to see what that PowerShell command contained," that's a coverage gap — escalate it, and turn a one-off frustration into a permanent fix.
